I love ice cream it's one of life's  simple pleasures but the science that's  much more complex at its core ice cream  is a product of phase transition in this  case water freezing into ice two things  that greatly impact the texture of ice  cream are the amount of the water in the  ice cream base that freezes and how  quickly that transition takes place so  one of the biggest challenges for the  home cook is making ice cream that is  dense smooth and free of noticeable  iciness what's interesting is that all  things being equal ice cream that we  perceive as icy doesn't actually contain  more ice crystals just bigger ones the  crystals and smooth ice cream are so  small that our tongues can't even detect  them there are two basic ways to prevent  ice cream from having an icy texture the  first is to control the water in the  base now this could mean reducing the  overall water content it could also mean  keeping more of the water in liquid form  by adjusting sweeteners sweeteners lower  the freezing point of the water in the  mixture preventing some of it from  turning to ice or you can use  ingredients like starches and gums which  trap free water and prevent it from  forming large ice crystals the second  way to prevent an icy texture is to make  sure that the ice crystals that do form  are as small as possible here we're  talking about rate of freezing the  faster you freeze ice cream the smaller  the ice crystals will be now commercial  ice cream manufacturers have a lot of  advantages over the home cook when it  comes to making ice cream they have  powerful stabilizers and professional  freezers and blast chillers that can  turn a liquid custard into a semi-solid  ice cream in a matter of seconds it's  not something you could do at home and  believe me an America's Test Kitchen we  try to so here are the ingredients for  our vanilla ice cream I'm using a  combination of heavy cream and milk for  the right level of richness that fat  also helps reduce the perception of ice  crystals by lubricating your tongue I'm  using fresh vanilla bean for its complex  flavor I'm going to add it early in the  process to get maximum flavor out of it  the next up we have the sweeteners which  have a huge impact on texture I'll come  back to that point in just a minute  here I'm using granulated sugar which is  pretty common and corn syrup which is  not a corn syrup is a combination of  sweet glucose molecules which make it  sweet and chains of broken corn starch  so it's these chains of tangled corn  starch which give corn syrup its  viscosity now in our ice cream they  prevent water molecules from group  freezing into big crystals so since  we're thinking about ice cream at the  moment let's imagine freezing it if we  cool it then we know that without the  sugar or salt the the temperature which  the the water becomes a solid at zero  degrees Celsius and that's because the  entropy of the water molecule is moving  around becomes balanced by the sticking  energy of water molecules to each other  to make the crystal if there's salt or  sugar in the water that salt or and  sugar has an additional entropy that is  besides the entropy of the water  molecules there's also an entropy of the  salt molecule so you can imagine these  little molecules jiggling around and  when you freeze the mixture you have to  also stop the motion of the solute  molecules of the sugar or salt and that  requires a little extra energy than you  would have had to give it otherwise so  this extra energy leads to the  phenomenon that people call freezing  point depression this is the notion that  if you actually try to freeze a mixture  of salt water or sugar water you have to  actually go to a lower temperature to  freeze it than you would if there were  no salt and sugar that lower temperature  is to actually put in the extra to get  rid of the extra energy that's needed to  actually stop to solute from moving  freezing point depression essentially  depends on the entropy of the solute and  as such what the amount that you have to  actually depress the freezing point  really depends on the number of solute  molecules per unit volume that you've  managed to put in the more you put in  the more the free the freezing point is  depressed if we think about this when  making ice cream it raises a bit of a  conundrum so if you make ice cream  you'll all recall or you will know soon  enough when you do the lab that the  recipe for ice cream essentially  involves taking milk which of course has  little fat globules and other things in  it it's basically water with fat  globules and other molecules and you  then pour sugar in the milk because you  would like to be sweet and then you need  to freeze the the sweet milk now if you  freeze the sweet milk by surrounding it  by just normal ice just normal frozen  water it won't freeze and the reason is  is because of freezing point depression  the mixture has a lower freezing point  than the water so somehow you have to  get the material that's around the milk  with the sugar in it to have a lower  freezing point  out of milk and a way to do that is to  add salt so if you add salt you of  course depress the freezing point of the  salt water if you then freeze that and  it will have a lower temperature than  the freezing point of the milk or a  sugar mixture and that will allow you to  freeze it and make ice cream so let's  let's see what it looks like so as you  can see it's nice and thick and dense  and I'll take a little try just to make  sure it's good and doesn't have any ice  that's good it's really nice and dense  very smooth and there's really no  noticeable ice crystals because we made  them nice and small so by understanding  and controlling the phase transition of  water to ice we get ice cream that's  scoop full right from the freezer into  dent its creamy and most importantly  perfectly smooth
 

